elius.github.io/post/2026-02-24-chartfold-owning-your-medical-records/</link><pubDate>Tue, 24 Feb 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-02-24-chartfold-owning-your-medical-records/</guid><description>A walkthrough of Chartfold, a Python tool that loads your medical records into SQLite and exposes them to Claude via MCP for structured analysis, visit prep, and ad-hoc queries.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-02-24-chartfold-owning-your-medical-records/analysis-cards.png" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Chartfold</media:title></media:content><category>chartfold</category><category>python</category><category>ehr</category><category>sqlite</category><category>health-data</category><category>open-source</category><category>mcp</category><category>claude</category></item><item><title>Code Without Purpose</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-02-25-code-without-purpose/</link><pubDate>Tue, 24 Feb 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-02-25-code-without-purpose/</guid><description>The problem isn&amp;#39;t too much code. It&amp;#39;s code without purpose.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-02-25-code-without-purpose/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Chartfold</media:title></media:content><category>longecho</category><category>open-source</category><category>software-philosophy</category><category>legacy</category></item></channel></rss>